## 4. Detailed I²C Sensor Pin Descriptions

### 4.1 VEML6075 — UV Sensor

| Pin | Description | Connection |
|---|---|---|
| VIN/VCC | Sensor supply | Use the voltage supported by the breakout |
| GND | Ground | Common GND |
| SDA | Serial data | GPIO4, example |
| SCL | Serial clock | GPIO5, example |

The VEML6075 measures UVA and UVB radiation and communicates measurement data digitally over I²C.

### 4.2 MPU6050 — Motion Sensor

| Pin | Description | Connection |
|---|---|---|
| VCC | Module power | Check the breakout's supply specification |
| GND | Ground | Common GND |
| SDA | Serial data | GPIO4, example |
| SCL | Serial clock | GPIO5, example |
| INT | Optional interrupt output | May be left disconnected if unused |

The MPU6050 contains a three-axis accelerometer and a three-axis gyroscope. It can provide motion and orientation-related measurements. An acceleration threshold alone does not constitute a validated fall-detection algorithm.

### 4.3 MLX90614 — Non-Contact Temperature Sensor

| Pin | Description | Connection |
|---|---|---|
| VIN/VCC | Sensor supply | Follow the breakout's voltage specification |
| GND | Ground | Common GND |
| SDA | Serial data | GPIO4, example |
| SCL | Serial clock | GPIO5, example |

The MLX90614 measures infrared radiation to estimate object temperature and also reports sensor ambient temperature. The result depends on the target, distance, field of view, and sensor configuration.

## 5. Required Voltage and Power Information

The voltage depends on whether the design uses a bare sensor IC or a breakout module containing a regulator and/or level shifting. The **actual module datasheet takes precedence**.

| Component | Supply guidance | Signal-level requirement |
|---|---|---|
| ESP32-C3 Mini | Use the board's specified USB or regulated input | GPIO logic is 3.3 V; do not apply 5 V to GPIOs |
| VEML6075 | Bare IC operates approximately within 2.4–3.6 V; verify breakout | I²C levels must be compatible with 3.3 V logic |
| MPU6050 | Bare IC supply is approximately 2.375–3.46 V; breakout requirements vary | Use compatible I²C logic levels |
| MLX90614 | Bare-device and breakout supply options vary by version | Confirm I²C pull-up voltage and module specification |
| MQ-7 module | Many common modules use a 5 V heater supply | AO must be conditioned to remain within ADC limits |
| MQ-135 module | Many common modules use a 5 V supply | AO must be conditioned to remain within ADC limits |
| LEDs | Driven from GPIO through individual resistors | Keep current within GPIO limits |
| Active buzzer | Depends on the buzzer model | Use a driver if its current exceeds safe GPIO capability |

### Power and Safety Rules

1. Never connect a 5 V signal directly to an ESP32-C3 GPIO or ADC input.
2. Measure each MQ module's AO voltage under operating conditions before connecting it to the ESP32.
3. Use a suitable voltage divider or other signal-conditioning circuit when required; calculate it for the maximum possible output voltage.
4. MQ heater circuits may consume substantially more current than small digital sensors. Do not assume the ESP32 board's 3.3 V regulator can power them.
5. Use an appropriately rated supply and connect grounds together for signals that share a reference.
6. Check the I²C breakout pull-ups. A module that pulls SDA/SCL to 5 V is not suitable for direct connection to 3.3 V ESP32 logic without appropriate level translation.

## 6. I²C Protocol Details

I²C (Inter-Integrated Circuit) is a synchronous, two-wire serial communication protocol. It allows several compatible devices to communicate with one controller using shared signal lines.

### 6.1 I²C Signal Lines

| Signal | Full name | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| SDA | Serial Data | Transfers addresses, commands, and sensor data |
| SCL | Serial Clock | Synchronizes communication |
| GND | Ground reference | Establishes the shared electrical reference |
| VCC/VIN | Power | Supplies the sensor module; not an I²C communication signal |

I²C lines are typically open-drain/open-collector and require pull-up resistors. The pull-up voltage must be compatible with every device connected to the bus.

### 6.2 How I²C Communication Works

1. The controller generates a START condition.
2. It sends the target device's address and a read/write bit.
3. The addressed device acknowledges when appropriate.
4. The controller and device exchange data bytes.
5. A receiver acknowledges each applicable byte.
6. The transaction ends with a STOP condition, or the controller may use a repeated START.

